{"prompt": "Is the staging server disk full? People are reporting failed uploads.", "response": "Let me check.\n\n> run: ssh staging df -h /var\n\n```\n/dev/sda2  40G  39.6G  0.4G  99% /var\n```\n\nYes — /var is at 99%. The biggest offender:\n\n> run: ssh staging du -sh /var/log/* | sort -rh | head -3\n\n```\n18G  /var/log/app/requests.log\n```\n\nAn unrotated request log. I'd truncate it and enable logrotate: `truncate -s 0 /var/log/app/requests.log`, then install the rotation config. Shall I proceed?"}
{"prompt": "What changed in the pricing module between v2.3 and v2.4?", "response": "Checking the history.\n\n> run: git log v2.3..v2.4 --oneline -- src/pricing/\n\n```\n8f21c4d fix: apply regional VAT after discounts\n3aa9e01 feat: volume tiers for enterprise SKUs\n```\n\nTwo changes: VAT is now applied after discounts (this changes totals for discounted EU orders), and enterprise SKUs gained volume tiers. The VAT fix is the one to flag in release notes."}
{"prompt": "Our /search API is timing out for complex queries. Can you check if the slow query log has any clues?", "response": "Pulling up the slow query log from the database server.\n\n> run: ssh db-primary 'grep /search /var/log/mysql/slow.log | tail -5'\n\n```\n# Query_time: 12.431 Lock_time: 0.001 rows_sent: 847 rows_examined: 2100000\nSELECT * FROM products WHERE MATCH(description) AGAINST(\"wireless bluetooth headset\") AND price BETWEEN 50 AND 200 ORDER BY relevance_score DESC;\n```\n\nFull table scan on 2.1M rows — the MATCH index isn't being used because the WHERE clause starts with a non-indexed condition. Reordering the WHERE clause to put MATCH first should help:\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM products WHERE MATCH(description) AGAINST(\"...\" ) AND price BETWEEN 50 AND 200 ORDER BY relevance_score DESC;\n```\n\nWant me to test this with EXPLAIN?"}
{"prompt": "The payment service container keeps crashing on deploy. Can you check the recent logs?", "response": "Checking the container logs.\n\n> run: docker logs --tail 30 payment-service 2>&1\n\n```\n2024-03-15T10:23:41Z ERROR failed to connect to vault: dial tcp 10.0.4.12:8200: connect: connection refused\n2024-03-15T10:23:41Z FATAL could not initialize secret store, aborting\nsignal: killed\n```\n\nThe payment service needs Vault for PCI-compliant key storage, but it's failing to reach the Vault endpoint at 10.0.4.12:8200. Let me verify the Vault pod is running:\n\n> run: kubectl get pods -n infra | grep vault\n\n```\nNAME                   READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE\nvault-0                0/1     CrashLoopBackOff   7          2h\n```\n\nVault itself is crashing. The payment service failure is downstream from that. Shall I dig into the Vault logs?"}
{"prompt": "I need to find all places in the codebase where we're using the old Stripe API key from env vars instead of the new secrets manager pattern.", "response": "Let me search for references to the old environment variable pattern.\n\n> run: grep -rn 'process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY' --include='*.ts' --include='*.js' src/\n\n```\nsrc/payments/charge.ts:14:  const stripe = Stripe(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY);\nsrc/payments/refund.ts:8:  const stripe = Stripe(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY);\nsrc/webhooks/handler.ts:22:  const stripe = Stripe(process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY);\nsrc/tests/payments.test.ts:45:  process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY = 'sk_test_mock';\n```\n\nThree production files need migrating:\n- `src/payments/charge.ts` — line 14\n- `src/payments/refund.ts` — line 8\n- `src/webhooks/handler.ts` — line 22\n\nThe test file is fine (mock key). The migration is straightforward: swap `process.env.STRIPE_SECRET_KEY` for `await secrets.get('stripe/secret_key')` in each file. Want me to generate the patches?"}
{"prompt": "Why is the CI build for PR #487 failing on the integration tests? The unit tests passed fine.", "response": "Let me pull up the CI logs for that PR.\n\n> run: gh run view --job=integration-tests --log 487 | grep -A 5 'FAIL\\|Error\\|fail:' | head -40\n\n```\nFAIL  tests/integration/checkout.test.ts (24.312s)\n  ● checkout flow › applies coupon correctly\n    expect(received).toBe(expected) // Object.is equality\n\n    Expected: 45.99\n    Received: 55.99\n\n  142 |     await expect(page.locator('#total')).toHaveText('$45.99');\n  143 |   });\n```\n\nThe coupon test expects $45.99 but gets $55.99 — a $10 difference. This suggests a coupon isn't being applied. Let me check if PR #487 touched the discount logic:\n\n> run: gh pr diff 487 --name-only | grep -i discount\n\n```\nsrc/cart/discounts.ts\n```\n\nYes — the PR modified `discounts.ts`. The test is likely exposing a regression in coupon application order. Want me to diff that file to spot the issue?"}
{"prompt": "Can you check if the CDN is caching our API responses? We just deployed a fix but users are still seeing stale data.", "response": "Let me check the response headers from the CDN for an API endpoint.\n\n> run: curl -sI https://cdn.example.com/api/v2/users/me | grep -iE 'cache|age|CDN|x-cdn'\n\n```\nX-Cache: HIT from edge-cdn-42\nX-Cache-Hits: 147\nage: 2340\nx-cdn-provider: cloudfront\n```\n\nConfirmed — the CDN is serving a cached response (HIT, 147 cache hits, age 2340s = ~39 minutes old). API responses should never be cached. Checking the CloudFront distribution settings:\n\n> run: aws cloudfront get-distribution-config --id E1ABC2DEF3 --query 'DistributionConfig.DefaultCacheBehavior'\n\n```\n{\n  \"ForwardedValues\": {\n    \"QueryString\": false,\n    \"Cookies\": { \"Forward\": \"none\" }\n  },\n  \"MinTTL\": 0,\n  \"DefaultTTL\": 86400,\n  \"MaxTTL\": 31536000\n}\n```\n\nThe origin is returning no `Cache-Control` headers, so CloudFront falls back to its DefaultTTL of 86400 seconds. The fix is to add `Cache-Control: no-store` to API responses at the origin. Want me to verify the origin response headers directly?"}
